1
00:00:00,230 --> 00:00:02,550
Hi, students. This video,

2
00:00:02,550 --> 00:00:05,110
I'll be talking about
processes and threads.

3
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:07,140
The objectives for this
video we're going to

4
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:10,065
learn and understand
about Linux processes,

5
00:00:10,065 --> 00:00:11,820
understand about Linux threads,

6
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:16,290
and also introduce inter-process
communication, or IPC.

7
00:00:16,290 --> 00:00:18,690
A Linux process is

8
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:21,535
executable object code
that's running on hardware.

9
00:00:21,535 --> 00:00:25,220
Often the encapsulation
of that object code is

10
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:26,765
in what's called ELF format

11
00:00:26,765 --> 00:00:29,185
or executable and
linkable format.

12
00:00:29,185 --> 00:00:31,055
This is what the,

13
00:00:31,055 --> 00:00:33,050
ultimately the binary
that you're using to

14
00:00:33,050 --> 00:00:35,680
execute and run on
any operating system,

15
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:37,315
but in the case of Linux,

16
00:00:37,315 --> 00:00:39,620
on Linux operating system.

17
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,440
For Linux process,

18
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:44,120
the necessary resources that are

19
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:46,070
needed to run are managed

20
00:00:46,070 --> 00:00:48,140
and allocated by
the kernel through

21
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,550
the system call
interface that we've

22
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:52,245
discussed in previous lectures.

23
00:00:52,245 --> 00:00:54,710
The first is timers

24
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:57,455
or files or any type
of hardware access,

25
00:00:57,455 --> 00:00:59,420
it's all organized and

26
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:03,145
managed by the kernel
through these system calls.

27
00:01:03,145 --> 00:01:06,560
All Linux processes
reference by a process ID.

28
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:07,910
That's something
that you can use

29
00:01:07,910 --> 00:01:09,470
to interact with the process,

30
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:13,325
to send it signals to
stop it, for instance.

31
00:01:13,325 --> 00:01:18,020
The process is going to
run on what's referred to

32
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:19,760
as a virtualized processor and

33
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:22,260
a virtualized view of
memory. What does that mean?

34
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:24,920
It means that from the
processes perspective,

35
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:28,445
it appears to have its own
dedicated RAM and CPU,

36
00:01:28,445 --> 00:01:30,500
it doesn't know about
any other processes

37
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,270
that are running in the system,

38
00:01:32,270 --> 00:01:36,270
but in reality it's being
time-shared by the kernel.

39
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:39,740
A visual depiction
here of what we mean

40
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:42,500
by having virtualized
processor and memory.

41
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:44,765
Again, from the
processes perspective,

42
00:01:44,765 --> 00:01:47,270
it just sees a chunk of
RAM that it has access to

43
00:01:47,270 --> 00:01:49,945
for the storage of its data,

44
00:01:49,945 --> 00:01:53,930
and it has access to a CPU
and it's executing over

45
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:55,340
time and it doesn't
know anything

46
00:01:55,340 --> 00:01:58,105
about any time-shifting
that's happening.

47
00:01:58,105 --> 00:02:01,430
But in reality, what's
happening is that the process

48
00:02:01,430 --> 00:02:02,990
RAM might be physically split

49
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:05,050
in different locations
in physical RAM,

50
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:08,315
and the process CPU is
getting time split with

51
00:02:08,315 --> 00:02:10,340
other processes that are
running on the system and

52
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:13,380
it's not really running
continuously in time.

53
00:02:14,230 --> 00:02:17,600
A process is made of
one or more threads,

54
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,130
and a thread is a unit of
activity within a process.

55
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:23,240
If you use, by default,

56
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:25,775
if you just compile a
Hello World application,

57
00:02:25,775 --> 00:02:28,025
you have one process and
you have one thread,

58
00:02:28,025 --> 00:02:31,160
and your application is
referred to as single-threaded.

59
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:33,770
If you specifically
start other threads,

60
00:02:33,770 --> 00:02:36,680
you'll be, as soon as you
start the second thread,

61
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:39,470
you'll be what's called a
multi-threaded application.

62
00:02:39,470 --> 00:02:41,300
In a multithreaded application,

63
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:43,250
every thread is going
to have its own stack,

64
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:45,065
which will be storing
local variables,

65
00:02:45,065 --> 00:02:47,720
it will be storing the
call stack that's used.

66
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:48,950
For instance, if you call

67
00:02:48,950 --> 00:02:51,990
a function or some
set of functions,

68
00:02:51,990 --> 00:02:53,270
that will be tracked inside

69
00:02:53,270 --> 00:02:55,895
the call stack for
that specific thread,

70
00:02:55,895 --> 00:02:57,980
and so it'll have
the processor state

71
00:02:57,980 --> 00:02:59,405
and the current location

72
00:02:59,405 --> 00:03:05,160
for a call tree in a specific
call stack for that thread.

73
00:03:05,690 --> 00:03:08,375
An important distinction between

74
00:03:08,375 --> 00:03:10,970
Linux threads and Linux
processes is that,

75
00:03:10,970 --> 00:03:12,440
well I mentioned before

76
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:14,930
every process has its
own virtualized view of

77
00:03:14,930 --> 00:03:15,950
memory and appears to have

78
00:03:15,950 --> 00:03:19,640
its own memory
isolated to itself.

79
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:21,650
The memory for
threads is shared,

80
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:23,434
so every thread within a process

81
00:03:23,434 --> 00:03:26,820
has exactly the
same memory space.

82
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:30,065
If every process has
its own virtual memory,

83
00:03:30,065 --> 00:03:33,500
but every thread
shares virtual memory,

84
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:36,520
then how would you share
memory access between threads?

85
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:37,835
If you wanted to access

86
00:03:37,835 --> 00:03:40,205
a global variable between
two different threads,

87
00:03:40,205 --> 00:03:42,800
you can just access
it directly, however,

88
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:45,440
you need to think about
if you're modifying

89
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:46,670
it from one thread and

90
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:48,170
you're reading it
from another thread,

91
00:03:48,170 --> 00:03:49,610
iIf you want to make
sure that you have

92
00:03:49,610 --> 00:03:51,740
a consistent view
of that memory,

93
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,380
you're going to have to
use synchronization,

94
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:56,825
and we'll talk about different
synchronization strategies

95
00:03:56,825 --> 00:03:59,230
later in the semester.

96
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:02,180
By comparison, if you're sharing

97
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:04,670
memory between
different processes,

98
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:06,920
that's not just built-in
like it is with threads,

99
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:08,180
because every process has

100
00:04:08,180 --> 00:04:10,330
its own virtualized
view of memory.

101
00:04:10,330 --> 00:04:11,900
You won't be able
to, for instance,

102
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:13,580
access a global
variable that's in

103
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:15,460
a different process by default.

104
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:18,370
However, there are different
mechanisms for doing that,

105
00:04:18,370 --> 00:04:20,750
and those are called
inter-process communication,

106
00:04:20,750 --> 00:04:22,610
which we'll cover a little
bit today and we'll get into

107
00:04:22,610 --> 00:04:25,380
a lot more detail
later in the semester.

108
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:29,050
The Linux signal is one of

109
00:04:29,050 --> 00:04:32,795
the simplest ways to do
inter-process communication.

110
00:04:32,795 --> 00:04:35,980
A signal is just a one-way
asynchronous notification

111
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:38,590
that can be sent from
the kernel to a process.

112
00:04:38,590 --> 00:04:41,140
It could be sent from one
process to another process,

113
00:04:41,140 --> 00:04:43,910
or it could be sent from
a process to itself.

114
00:04:43,910 --> 00:04:46,270
The process can set up

115
00:04:46,270 --> 00:04:47,485
a signal handler that

116
00:04:47,485 --> 00:04:49,540
controls how a signal
is responded to.

117
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:52,285
There's a chunk of code
you can define that says,

118
00:04:52,285 --> 00:04:55,530
if a specific signal
occurs, do this action.

119
00:04:55,530 --> 00:04:57,805
For example, a common signal

120
00:04:57,805 --> 00:04:59,720
that you might want to
handle will be Control C.

121
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:01,855
If you've ever run
an application on

122
00:05:01,855 --> 00:05:04,285
a Linux terminal before
and wanted to stop,

123
00:05:04,285 --> 00:05:06,190
you've probably use
the Control C key.

124
00:05:06,190 --> 00:05:07,870
What you're doing
there is sending

125
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:10,400
a SIGINT to stop the process,

126
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,020
and your code can had

127
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:14,540
a signal handler that
handles that SIGINT and

128
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:19,560
does things like shutdown
resources and gracefully close.

129
00:05:20,090 --> 00:05:23,690
If you write a signal handler
or use a signal handler,

130
00:05:23,690 --> 00:05:25,250
you must use signal say,

131
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:26,855
functions that are safe to call

132
00:05:26,855 --> 00:05:28,770
asynchronously. Why is that?

133
00:05:28,770 --> 00:05:30,800
It's because there's
no guarantee

134
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:32,645
of any synchronous capability

135
00:05:32,645 --> 00:05:34,520
when you hit Control C

136
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:36,860
relative to what's running
in your current code.

137
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:39,800
If you're calling
some function in

138
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:42,470
your running process and you hit

139
00:05:42,470 --> 00:05:43,760
Control C and your signal

140
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:45,620
handler calls that
same function,

141
00:05:45,620 --> 00:05:46,970
you need to make sure that,

142
00:05:46,970 --> 00:05:48,470
that function is safe to call in

143
00:05:48,470 --> 00:05:50,400
two different contexts at once.

144
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:53,960
One of the easiest ways
to do that would be to,

145
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:55,370
or one of the easiest
ways to break

146
00:05:55,370 --> 00:05:58,765
that capability would be to
access global variables.

147
00:05:58,765 --> 00:06:01,310
If you're accessing
global variables in

148
00:06:01,310 --> 00:06:03,290
a non consistent way

149
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:06,650
between two different functions
that are asynchronous,

150
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:07,910
that wouldn't be a safe thing

151
00:06:07,910 --> 00:06:09,865
to do from a signal handler.

152
00:06:09,865 --> 00:06:12,320
You have to keep in mind
when you write these,

153
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:13,940
that process code can
be interrupted at

154
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:16,010
anytime by a signal

155
00:06:16,010 --> 00:06:19,140
and your signal handler could
be invoked at any time.

156
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:23,120
IPC, more generically,

157
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:25,385
or Linux processing
or communication,

158
00:06:25,385 --> 00:06:27,530
allows the processes to exchange

159
00:06:27,530 --> 00:06:30,120
information without having
that global memory space.

160
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:32,825
Processes keep their own
virtualized view of memory,

161
00:06:32,825 --> 00:06:35,260
but they can still exchange
information with each other.

162
00:06:35,260 --> 00:06:38,570
There are several
different IPC mechanisms

163
00:06:38,570 --> 00:06:39,920
that we'll get into

164
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:41,270
more detail about a couple

165
00:06:41,270 --> 00:06:42,560
of these later in the semester.

166
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:45,230
But pipes, semaphores,
message queues,

167
00:06:45,230 --> 00:06:47,000
and shared memory
are all examples of

168
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:47,870
ways that you can do

169
00:06:47,870 --> 00:06:50,310
inter-process
communication in Linux.